WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.095
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEIC preparation.

2
00:00:05.395 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:16.970
Unit eighteen: Part 7: Multiple Passages. Today you will connect information across two or three related texts.

4
00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:20.003
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:24.130
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:26.019
Passage.

7
00:00:27.017 --> 00:00:29.028
Passage means a section of text.

8
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:31.303
Read both passages.

9
00:00:31.917 --> 00:00:34.105
Your turn. Say: Passage.

10
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:37.875
Passage.

11
00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:39.339
Link.

12
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.966
Link means to connect.

13
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.027
Link the two texts.

14
00:00:44.617 --> 00:00:47.083
Your turn. Say: Link.

15
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:50.818
Link.

16
00:00:51.583 --> 00:00:52.863
Cross-reference.

17
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:57.510
Cross-reference means to compare information from different texts.

18
00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:00.453
Cross-reference the email and the form.

19
00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.167
Your turn. Say: Cross-reference.

20
00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.777
Cross-reference.

21
00:01:09.517 --> 00:01:10.483
Confirm.

22
00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:14.029
Confirm means to show that something is true.

23
00:01:14.617 --> 00:01:16.686
The second text confirms the date.

24
00:01:17.283 --> 00:01:19.947
Your turn. Say: Confirm.

25
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:23.859
Confirm.

26
00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:25.734
Contradict.

27
00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.935
Contradict means to say the opposite.

28
00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.968
The reply contradicts the first email.

29
00:01:32.583 --> 00:01:35.503
Your turn. Say: Contradict.

30
00:01:38.367 --> 00:01:39.310
Contradict.

31
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:41.028
Reply.

32
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:44.167
Reply means an answer to a message.

33
00:01:44.783 --> 00:01:46.191
Read the reply.

34
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:49.296
Your turn. Say: Reply.

35
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:53.181
Reply.

36
00:01:53.917 --> 00:01:54.941
Invoice.

37
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.450
Invoice means a bill.

38
00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.817
The invoice shows the price.

39
00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:02.895
Your turn. Say: Invoice.

40
00:02:05.733 --> 00:02:06.792
Invoice.

41
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:08.528
Timetable.

42
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:11.760
Timetable means a table of times.

43
00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:13.815
Check the timetable.

44
00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:17.023
Your turn. Say: Timetable.

45
00:02:19.867 --> 00:02:20.833
Timetable.

46
00:02:21.633 --> 00:02:25.156
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:28.771
Visal, what are multiple-passage questions?

48
00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:39.165
They are based on two or three related texts, such as an email and a schedule. At least one question needs information from more than one text.

49
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.017
How do I solve those questions?

50
00:02:43.117 --> 00:02:53.664
Find a clue in the first text, such as a date or name, then locate matching information in the second text. The answer is usually the connection between them.

51
00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.030
How do I save time?

52
00:02:57.117 --> 00:03:06.375
Do not read all texts in detail at the start. Read the question, find the first clue, and then check the other text for the second clue.

53
00:03:07.533 --> 00:03:09.127
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:09.983 --> 00:03:14.212
Grammar focus: Strategy: clue in one text, answer in another.

55
00:03:14.512 --> 00:03:15.699
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:16.383 --> 00:03:20.705
The email says the flight leaves at noon; the timetable shows gate B.

57
00:03:21.617 --> 00:03:24.766
The reply contradicts the earlier message about the date.

58
00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:32.509
Find the first clue in one text and the matching detail in the other. Check whether the second text confirms or changes the first.

59
00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:36.912
Be careful! Don't say: I use only one text.

60
00:03:38.867 --> 00:03:41.261
Say: I link the two texts.

61
00:03:41.561 --> 00:03:43.084
Use both texts.

62
00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:46.320
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:47.133 --> 00:03:48.727
Email and Timetable.

64
00:03:49.027 --> 00:03:52.048
A manager emails her team that her flight leaves at noon.

65
00:03:53.033 --> 00:03:55.497
A second text is the airport timetable.

66
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.952
It shows that the noon flight leaves from Gate B.

67
00:03:59.967 --> 00:04:02.454
A question asks where the manager should go.

68
00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:06.198
The answer needs information from both texts.

69
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.279
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:12.117 --> 00:04:14.476
Multiple-passage questions use blank.

71
00:04:14.776 --> 00:04:19.091
A, more than one text. B, one word. C, no text.

72
00:04:22.633 --> 00:04:25.469
The answer is A: more than one text.

73
00:04:26.617 --> 00:04:29.081
The timetable shows the flight leaves from blank.

74
00:04:29.381 --> 00:04:32.930
A, Gate Z. B, Gate B. C, Gate A.

75
00:04:36.467 --> 00:04:38.420
The answer is B: Gate B.

76
00:04:39.583 --> 00:04:41.478
To cross-reference is to blank.

77
00:04:41.778 --> 00:04:46.651
A, skip texts. B, copy texts. C, compare texts.

78
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.432
The answer is C: compare texts.

79
00:04:53.583 --> 00:04:55.235
What does the email say?

80
00:04:55.535 --> 00:05:00.999
A, the flight leaves at noon. B, the flight is cancelled. C, the flight is free.

81
00:05:04.533 --> 00:05:07.543
The answer is A: the flight leaves at noon.

82
00:05:08.683 --> 00:05:10.358
To save time you should blank.

83
00:05:10.658 --> 00:05:15.356
A, guess. B, start from the question. C, read all details first.

84
00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.654
The answer is B: start from the question.

85
00:05:22.867 --> 00:05:25.879
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:26.717 --> 00:05:29.529
How do you answer a question that needs two texts?

87
00:05:38.317 --> 00:05:39.921
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:40.221 --> 00:05:55.218
I read the question and find the first clue in one text, such as a name or a time. Then I look for the matching information in the second text. I check whether it confirms or changes the first clue and then choose the answer.

89
00:05:55.518 --> 00:05:57.157
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:04.104
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: clue in one text, answer in another.

91
00:06:04.404 --> 00:06:09.123
Today's value is analytical thinking. Look for the pattern underneath the surface.

92
00:06:09.423 --> 00:06:12.087
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

93
00:06:12.387 --> 00:06:14.819
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

